<preface | About this Monograph>
This monograph is a believable miniature: it exercises every alpha feature in
one document so the alpha integration check has a single cross-feature artifact
to point at.<note | A preface footnote — it collects at the end of the preface book-part under the book's default note-scope=chapter.>

<chapter | Foundations>
The pitch of a note is its fundamental frequency, measured in hertz. Concert A
is $440$ Hz, and the perfect fifth above it stands in the ratio $3/2$.

<definition #def:interval | An *interval* is the ratio $f_2/f_1$ of the frequencies of two pitches.>

<theorem #thm:octave | Doubling a frequency raises a pitch by exactly one octave.>

<proof | An octave is the 2:1 frequency ratio, so doubling a frequency is one octave by definition. ∎>

The equal-tempered scale divides the octave into twelve equal ratios:

<$$ #eqn:equal | f_n = f_0 \cdot 2^{n/12} $$>

Benson's treatment <cite @Benson2007> develops this from first principles. A
reference to <ref @thm:octave> resolves to the theorem above.<note | A foundations footnote, collected at the end of chapter 1.>

<fig #fig:keyboard src=keyboard.png | One octave of a piano keyboard.>

<chapter | Consonance and Ratios>
<author | Guest Author>
This chapter has its own author — the edited-volume case — distinct from the
book-level author.

Small whole-number ratios sound consonant. The just-intonation intervals are
collected in <ref @tab:ratios>.

<csv #tab:ratios caption="Just-intonation interval ratios.">
interval,ratio,cents
unison,1/1,0
fifth,3/2,702
fourth,4/3,498
octave,2/1,1200
</csv>

The fifth and the fourth are the two principal consonances:

<align #eqn:ratios>
r_{fifth} &= 3/2 \\
r_{fourth} &= 4/3
</align>

Sethares <cite @Sethares1993> ties consonance to timbre rather than to ratios
alone.<note | A consonance footnote, collected at the end of chapter 2.>

<chapter | Visual and Aural Structure>
Two external notations close the body. A flow of the analysis pipeline:

<mermaid #fig:circle caption="From pitch to perceived interval.">
graph LR
  A[Pitch pair] --> B[Frequency ratio]
  B --> C[Cents]
  C --> D[Perceived interval]
</mermaid>

And a short tune in ABC notation:

<abc #fig:tune caption="Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star.">
X:1
T:Twinkle Twinkle
M:4/4
L:1/4
K:C
C C G G | A A G2 | F F E E | D D C2 |
</abc>

Cross-references <ref @fig:circle> and <ref @fig:tune> resolve to the two
figures in this chapter.<note | A structure footnote, collected at the end of chapter 3.>

<appendix | Notation and Sources>
This appendix lives in book-back. The interval names follow the usual Western
convention; the frequency model is the equal-tempered one of <ref @eqn:equal>.<note | An appendix footnote, collected at the end of the appendix book-part.>
